========================================================================
COLLISIONLAYERS
========================================================================
( [T0] -- Shadow gets its OWN layer, separate from Crate. That's not   )
( an accident - [T6]'s rule needs a Crate and a Shadow to be able to   )
( occupy the SAME cell at once, so it can catch that overlap and       )
( react to it. If they shared a layer they could never overlap in the )
( first place, and the rule would never have anything to match.        )

Background
Exit, Sign, SavePoint, Lava, Trophy, Guard
Shadow
Wall, Player, Crate

========================================================================
RULES
========================================================================

( [T3] the in-RULE "message" command -------------------------------- )
( This is a completely different mechanism from the LEVELS-section     )
( MESSAGE you'll meet in [T1]/[T2] below. "message" is a COMMAND you   )
( tack onto the end of a rule's output, same slot "again" lives in.    )
( It pops a text screen, same renderer and all, but it does NOT        )
( advance to the next level - the current level just resumes, exactly )
( as it was, once you dismiss it. It reads everything to the right of  )
( the word "message" as the text, so it has to be the LAST thing on    )
( the line. )

[ Player Sign ] -> [ Player Sign ] message This popped from a RULE, not a level - reach the Exit and compare.

( Nothing here removes Sign or replaces it with anything, so walking   )
( onto it again re-shows the same message every time - useful for a    )
( reusable in-world hint sign, as opposed to a one-shot trigger.        )
( -- next: [T4] )

( [T4] CHECKPOINT: a rule-triggered save point ------------------------ )
( [T5] RESTART: a rule-triggered "press R" ----------------------------- )
( Both commands below are combined with a normal keyword pattern         )
( exactly the way "message" was above - they just go in the same slot. )

late [ Player SavePoint ] -> CHECKPOINT message Checkpoint set - RESTART now returns you here, not to the level start.

[ Player Lava ] -> [ Player Lava ] restart

( CHECKPOINT saves the live game state the moment this late rule sees  )
( Player standing on SavePoint - late, so it's checking where you      )
( ACTUALLY ended up this turn, not where you were about to go. From    )
( that point on, both the RESTART command above and the player's own R )
( key stop returning to the level's opening layout and instead reload  )
( from that saved state. Touch Lava before ever touching SavePoint and )
( restart sends you all the way back to the start, same as always -    )
( touch SavePoint first and THEN Lava, and it drops you back on the    )
( SavePoint tile instead. Nothing about the R key itself changed; what )
( changed is which state counts as "the beginning" for as long as this )
( browser tab's checkpoint sticks around. -- next: [T6] )

( [T6] CANCEL: reverting an entire turn -------------------------------- )
( A plain Wall stops movement by being physically solid - it can only   )
( ever block, unconditionally, every time. CANCEL does something a      )
( collision layer structurally can't: it reacts to a CONDITION after    )
( the fact, and when that condition is met, it throws away everything   )
( that happened this turn - not just the one piece that triggered it. )

late [ Crate Shadow ] -> CANCEL

( This says nothing about Player at all - it only fires if a Crate      )
( would end up sharing a cell with Shadow. But push a Crate toward a    )
( Shadow tile and your OWN forward step gets undone right along with    )
( the push, even though Player never touched Shadow directly - because )
( late checks the position after the whole turn resolved, and CANCEL    )
( doesn't undo "the push," it undoes "the turn." That's a strictly      )
( more powerful tool than solidity for anything conditional: a one-way  )
( gate, a "you can walk here but you can't push crates here" zone, a    )
( trap that only springs if you're carrying something. -- next: [T7] )

( [T7] WIN: winning straight from a rule ------------------------------- )
( WINCONDITIONS below is the usual "Some Player on Exit" - but that's   )
( checked for every level in this file, including the one after this   )
( comment, which has no Exit tile on its map at all. The ONLY way that  )
( level can end is this: )

[ Player Trophy ] -> [ Player Trophy ] win

( -- next: [T9], since [T8] has no RULES-section entry - it's a        )
( Prelude switch, see the top of this file and the [T8] level below. )

( [T9] combining a normal rewrite with a trailing command --------------- )
( Every command so far sat on a right side that just repeated the left )
( side unchanged - [ Player Sign ] -> [ Player Sign ] message ... left  )
( both objects untouched and only added a command. Commands can just    )
( as easily ride along on a rule that ALSO moves something for real: )

[ > Player | Guard ] -> [ < Player | Guard ] message Halt! No further.

( No direction keyword out front, so [as covered back in the first      )
( tutorial's T4] this expands to all four compass directions - approach )
( Guard from any side and the same thing happens. The pattern reads     )
( "Player moving toward Guard" and rewrites it to "Player moving away    )
( from Guard" - so instead of just stopping at the wall the way Guard's )
( own solidity already would, Player visibly recoils, and the message   )
( rides along on the exact same rule that caused the recoil. )

========================================================================
WINCONDITIONS
========================================================================
( [T0] -- global for the whole file, checked after every level. Every  )
( level needs an Exit tile to win normally EXCEPT [T7]'s, which wins    )
( entirely through the "win" command in RULES above instead.            )

Some Player on Exit

