LEVELS
========================================================================

( [T1] -- a plain room. The MESSAGE line right under this map, with no )
( blank line in between, is ATTACHED to this specific level - it fires )
( the instant you reach the Exit and this level's win-condition is met.)
#######
#P....#
#....d#
#######
MESSAGE [T1]: that was an ATTACHED message - a MESSAGE line sitting directly beneath one level's map, shown the moment THAT level's WINCONDITIONS are satisfied.

( [T2] -- two completely standalone MESSAGE screens, back to back,     )
( with blank lines around each and no map behind either one. Each is   )
( its own entry in the level sequence, dismissed independently. )

MESSAGE [T2] part 1 of 2: this screen has no map behind it at all - a MESSAGE line standing alone between blank lines becomes its own screen in the sequence, same as a level would.

MESSAGE [T2] part 2 of 2: and here's a second one, immediately after the first. Consecutive standalone MESSAGE lines just queue up as separate screens, each waiting on its own X to continue.

( [T3] -- Sign (n) pops a message the moment you step on it, WITHOUT   )
( ending the level. Reach the Exit afterward to see this level's own   )
( attached recap fire separately. )
#########
#P..n...#
#.......#
#......d#
#########
MESSAGE [T3]: that popup came from an in-RULE "message" command, not from a level's own MESSAGE line - and unlike this recap, it didn't end anything. Walk back onto the Sign and it'll show again; nothing consumed it.

( [T4]/[T5] -- SavePoint (s) sets a checkpoint; Lava (k) restarts the  )
( level. Try walking straight into the Lava first and watch R/RESTART  )
( send you all the way back to P's start. Then reset, touch the        )
( SavePoint, THEN the Lava, and compare where you land. )
##################
#P...s.......k..d#
##################
MESSAGE [T4]/[T5]: touching Lava before the SavePoint restarted you at the very start, same as always. Touch the SavePoint first, though, and Lava's restart drops you back on top of it instead - CHECKPOINT quietly moved the target that both RESTART and your own R key aim at.

( [T6] -- an open room, not a corridor, so the Exit is always reachable )
( whether or not you ever touch the Crate (C). Push it toward the      )
( Shadow (h) tile on purpose and watch your own step get undone right  )
( along with the push. )
#############
#P.C.h......#
#...........#
#..........d#
#############
MESSAGE [T6]: pushing the Crate onto the Shadow tile cancelled the whole turn - your own forward step included - even though you never touched Shadow yourself. late+CANCEL reverts everything that happened that turn, not just the one collision that triggered it.

( [T7] -- no Exit tile anywhere on this map. The Trophy (t) is the     )
( only way out. )
#######
#P..t.#
#######
MESSAGE [T7]: that win came entirely from the "win" command sitting on Trophy's rule - there was no Exit on that map for the global "Some Player on Exit" WINCONDITIONS to ever satisfy.

( [T8] -- noundo / norestart are Prelude-wide switches, so there's no  )
( level that can demonstrate them in isolation the way every other tag )
( here does. This room is just a placeholder for the recap. )
#########
#P......#
#.......#
#......d#
#########
MESSAGE [T8]: scroll up to the Prelude, right under "author," and uncomment noundo or norestart - reload the page and Z or R will stop responding, everywhere in the file, not just in one level. Re-comment them before you keep exploring, or undo/restart stay off for the rest of this session.

( [T9] -- Guard (g) knocks Player back and shows a message in ONE      )
( rule. Approach from any side; the recoil works in all four           )
( directions since the rule never specified one. )
###########
#P..g.....#
#.........#
#........d#
###########
MESSAGE [T9]: Guard's rule did two things at once - reversed Player's movement AND popped a message - because a trailing command can ride along on a rule that's also doing a real rewrite, not just on a pass-through rule like Sign's back in [T3].

( [T10] -- grab bag, standalone screens, no map. )

MESSAGE [T10] grab bag, part 1: default keys during play are arrows or WASD to move; Z, U, or Ctrl+Z to undo; R to restart; X, Enter, Space, or C for action (and to dismiss a message like this one); Escape returns to the title screen.

MESSAGE [T10] grab bag, part 2: add a line that just says "debug" up in the Prelude (near "title") to see the exact compiled rule instructions in the console on RUN. "verbose_logging" does the same for CHECKPOINT/AGAIN/restart events while you actually play, not just at compile time.

MESSAGE [T10] grab bag, part 3: MESSAGE text auto-wraps at 34 characters wide and centers itself, but the screen only fits 12 wrapped lines - go past that and the compiler still runs your game, it just warns "Message too long to fit on screen" and truncates. Keep popups short.

MESSAGE [T10] grab bag, part 4 - a real one: the docs claim key_repeat_interval defaults to 1.5 seconds. The engine's actual default is 0.15 seconds - a straight typo in the documentation, not a fork difference or a setting you're missing. If PuzzleScript's default key-repeat has ever felt snappier than advertised, that's why.

MESSAGE That's the whole file. Two more of these live in this repo for the PuzzleScript+ and PuzzleScript Next forks specifically - go compare what a fork-only level-select menu and pause screen add on top of everything above.
